= Rehmannia (地黃)

_A single plant —_ Rehmannia glutinosa _(Gaertn.) Libosch. ex Fisch. & C. A. Mey. — treated by classical Chinese medicine as_ four distinct drug-forms _by preparation. The substance-by-saṃskāra paradigm of the ginger and tangerine-peel entries reaches its most-developed expression in rehmannia: the_ four forms — fresh / dried / wine-prepared / charred — _have_ different properties on every dravyaguna axis, _from cold-blood-cooling to warm-yin-tonifying, on the same root. Status: Chinese — fully canonical, among the most-used drugs of the_ Shennong Bencao Jing _superior class; the cornerstone of the_ Liver-Kidney-yin-blood-supplementing _category and the foundation of countless major formulas including_ Liu Wei Di Huang Wan_, the most-prescribed Chinese formula globally. Ayurveda: not classical (East Asian species). Tibb: not classical. Greco-Roman: not classical._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Rehmannia glutinosa_ (Gaertn.) Libosch. ex Fisch. & C. A. Mey., family Orobanchaceae (formerly Scrophulariaceae — the species was reclassified in 21st-c. molecular phylogenetics). _Rehmannia_ commemorates *Joseph Rehmann* (1788–1831), German-Russian physician resident in St. Petersburg. _Glutinosa_ = "glutinous, sticky" — the root's characteristic sticky-mucilaginous texture, which is the defining handling property of the drug. The drug is the *root tuber* — a thickened, fleshy root, golden-brown externally and yellow internally in the fresh state, blackening during the wine-preparation process.

*English:* _Rehmannia_ (the genus name retained as the principal English drug name); also *Chinese foxglove* (from the historical Scrophulariaceae classification with foxglove _Digitalis_; the species was thought related, and the flowers do resemble a foxglove) — though the modern reclassification has separated the genera. *Di Huang* is the standard romanization in modern Western herbal use.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "rehmannia"); the genus dedication: Burkhardt, _Eponymisches Wörterbuch der Botanik_. The Orobanchaceae reclassification: APG IV phylogeny (2016) and subsequent molecular literature.]

*Chinese — the canonical four drug-form distinctions:*

The plant yields *four distinct drugs by saṃskāra* — fresh, dried, wine-prepared, and charred. The four are treated as functionally separate substances and are not interchangeable in classical formulas. This is the most-developed single-plant drug-form differentiation in Chinese pharmacy.

- *鮮地黃 (xiān dìhuáng, "fresh rehmannia")* — the *fresh just-harvested root tuber*; cold; for severe heat-pattern blood diseases. Rarely encountered outside the harvest region because of its perishability.
- *生地黃 (shēng dìhuáng, "raw / dried rehmannia")* — the *fresh root dried* under controlled conditions (sun-dried, oven-dried, or processed by traditional charring-and-roasting cycles). Sometimes also called *乾地黃 (gān dìhuáng, "dry rehmannia")*. Cold property; for heat-pattern blood diseases, the canonical Cool-the-Blood drug; the basis of 增液湯 and similar yin-fluid-supplementing formulas.
- *熟地黃 (shú dìhuáng, "prepared / cooked rehmannia")* — the dried root *steamed with rice wine (黃酒)* in a multi-cycle process (the canonical *九蒸九晒 jiǔ zhēng jiǔ shài*, "nine steamings and nine sun-dryings") until *glossy black* throughout. The transformation is dramatic — the fresh-yellow becomes deep-black, the bitter-cool becomes sweet-warming, the cooling-blood-clearing becomes the warming-yin-blood-tonifying. *The 熟地黃 transformation is one of the cleanest examples of saṃskāra-creates-substance in the entire materia medica.*
- *地黃炭 (dìhuáng tàn, "charred rehmannia")* — root charred until the surface is black and the interior brown; bitter-astringent; for severe bleeding patterns.

The canonical Chinese pharmacy regards these as four distinct substances; substitution of one for another in a classical formula constitutes a significant prescription error.#footnote[地黃 four-form classification: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004), with separate entries for 生地黃 and 熟地黃 (the two principal medicinal forms); 鮮地黃 and 地黃炭 as variant preparations. The 九蒸九晒 (nine steamings and nine sun-dryings) process is one of the most-elaborated processing methods in classical Chinese pharmacy.]

The *Huaiqing 懷慶 (modern Henan 河南) production region* is the canonical origin — rehmannia is one of the *四大懷藥 (Sì Dà Huái Yào, "Four Great Huaiqing Drugs")*, alongside *山藥 (Chinese yam, Dioscorea opposita)*, *牛膝 (Achyranthes bidentata)*, and *菊花 (chrysanthemum)*. The Huaiqing-grown rehmannia is GI-protected and commercially distinguished.

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry — _Rehmannia glutinosa_ is exclusively Chinese / Korean / Japanese. Modern Hindi: no standardized name.

*Arabic / Persian:* No classical Tibb entry. Modern Arabic: ريهمانية (_rīhmāniyya_) — direct transliteration; the substance has limited adoption in modern Arabic-language herbal literature, with no classical attestation.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

地黃 — in all four forms — is among the most-used drugs of the entire Chinese pharmacopoeia; from the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ superior class (上品), through Zhang Zhongjing's _Shanghan Lun_ and _Jīnguì Yàolüè_, through the foundational Song-period prescription compendia, into modern Chinese pharmacy practice.

=== 鮮地黃 (Fresh rehmannia)

*Property (性):* Cold (寒). *Flavor (味):* Sweet (甘), bitter (苦). *Channels (歸經):* Heart (心), Liver (肝), Kidney (腎).

*Actions:*
- 清熱涼血 — clears heat and cools the blood; for severe heat-pattern bleeding (nosebleed, hematemesis, hematuria from heat-toxin).
- 養陰生津 — nourishes yin and generates fluids; for severe febrile yin-fluid depletion.
- 涼血止血 — cools blood and stops bleeding.

Used principally in *Wen Bing* (warm-disease) acute conditions — Yu Xichang's tradition and the Qing-period Wen Bing School favored the fresh form for the most severe ying-and-blood-level heat. Modern clinical use is limited by perishability; the dried 生地黃 is the typical substitute.

=== 生地黃 (Raw / dried rehmannia)

*Property:* Cold (寒). *Flavor:* Sweet (甘), bitter (苦). *Channels:* Heart, Liver, Kidney.

*Actions:*
- 清熱涼血 — clears heat and cools the blood (the canonical use); for heat-pattern bleeding, skin eruptions, fever rashes.
- 養陰生津 — nourishes yin and generates fluids; for yin-fluid depletion with thirst, constipation, dry mouth.
- 止血 — stops bleeding from heat patterns.

*Used in:* febrile diseases with bleeding; chronic yin-vacuity with low-grade fever (_diabetic-spectrum 消渴 xiāokě_); constipation from fluid depletion; menorrhagia from heat; the foundational *Cool the Blood* category.

*Standard formulas:* *四物湯 (Sì Wù Tāng, "Four Substances Decoction")* uses 生地黃 (or 熟地黃 in tonifying-blood variants); *清營湯 (Qīng Yíng Tāng, "Clear the Ying Decoction")* for Wen Bing ying-level heat; *增液湯 (Zēng Yè Tāng, "Increase the Fluids Decoction")* for fluid-depletion constipation.

*Cautions:* Spleen-vacuity-damp with loose stools, qi-vacuity with poor appetite (the cold cloying nature aggravates digestive vacuity).#footnote[生地黃 entry: _Shennong Bencao Jing_; _Bencao Gangmu_ 地黃 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

=== 熟地黃 (Prepared / cooked rehmannia)

*Property:* Slightly warm (微溫) — *the saṃskāra has transformed the cold-property to warming*. *Flavor:* Sweet (甘) — more strongly so than the raw form. *Channels:* Liver (肝), Kidney (腎) — *with the Heart channel dropped and the Kidney channel intensified*; the channel scope has *narrowed and deepened* compared with 生地黃.

*Actions:*
- 滋陰補血 — *enriches yin and supplements blood* — the cardinal action; for Liver-and-Kidney yin-and-blood vacuity. The most-used 補血 (blood-supplementing) drug in Chinese pharmacy.
- 益精填髓 — *augments essence and fills the marrow*; for Kidney-essence depletion with bone weakness, premature aging, infertility.
- 補腎滋肝 — tonifies Kidney and nourishes Liver.

The transformation from 生 to 熟 is dramatic on every axis: cold → warm; bitter-sweet → predominantly sweet; cooling-blood-clearing → warming-yin-blood-tonifying; broad channel scope → deep Liver-Kidney; acute febrile use → chronic vacuity use. *This is the cleanest case of saṃskāra-creates-different-drug in Chinese pharmacy* — the transformed drug is functionally a different substance from the raw root.

*The standard formula:* *六味地黃丸 (Liù Wèi Dì Huáng Wán, "Six-Ingredient Rehmannia Pill")* — 熟地黃 + 山茱萸 + 山藥 + 澤瀉 + 茯苓 + 牡丹皮; *the most-prescribed Chinese formula globally*. Originally from *錢乙 Qián Yǐ's _Xiǎo Ér Yào Zhèng Zhí Jué_ (小兒藥證直訣, 1119)*, a pediatric formulary; subsequently became the foundational adult Liver-Kidney-yin tonic. Many derivative formulas: *知柏地黃丸* (with anemarrhena and phellodendron, for yin-vacuity with empty-heat); *杞菊地黃丸* (with goji and chrysanthemum, for Liver-Kidney yin-vacuity with eye symptoms); *麥味地黃丸* (with ophiopogon and schisandra, for Kidney-yin-vacuity with cough); *桂附地黃丸 / 金匱腎氣丸* (with cinnamon and aconite, the "Kidney qi pill" for Kidney-yang vacuity).

*Cautions:* Spleen-vacuity with loose stools / damp / phlegm — the sticky-cloying nature aggravates the Spleen. The classical pairing with *砂仁 (sand cardamom)* or *ginger* in compound formulas counteracts the cloying tendency.#footnote[熟地黃 and 六味地黃丸: 六味地黃丸 from Qian Yi, _Xiǎo Ér Yào Zhèng Zhí Jué_ (小兒藥證直訣, 1119), originally for pediatric Kidney-yin-vacuity; subsequently became the foundational adult formula. The 桂附地黃丸 / 金匱腎氣丸 is from Zhang Zhongjing's _Jīnguì Yàolüè_, the historical predecessor formula. The derivative-formula tree of Liu Wei Di Huang Wan is one of the largest in Chinese pharmacy.]

=== 地黃炭 (Charred rehmannia)

*Property:* Slightly warm. *Flavor:* Bitter, astringent, slightly sweet. *Channels:* Liver, Kidney.

*Actions:* 涼血止血 — cools blood and stops bleeding (the bitter-astringent character intensifies the blood-stopping action). Used principally for *severe bleeding patterns* — uterine bleeding, postpartum hemorrhage, severe menorrhagia.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Rehmannia is_ not classical in the Sanskrit canon — _the species is exclusively East Asian (Chinese, Korean, Japanese cultivation and origin). No saṃhitā-canonical entry; no nighaṇṭu entry. Modern Ayurvedic adoption is post-classical and minimal._

*Reasoned cross-system placement* (anchored on the Chinese readings, by drug-form):

*生地黃 (raw):* tikta-kaṣāya (bitter-astringent) rasa with madhura (sweet) secondary; guru-snigdha guṇa; *śīta vīrya* (the strongest cooling of the four forms); madhura vipāka. Pacifies Pitta strongly; aggravates Kapha and Vāta in excess. _Rakta-prasādana_ (blood-clarifying — converges with the Chinese 涼血), _dāha-praśamana_ (relieves burning sensations), _pittahara_, _rakta-pitta-hara_ (anti-bleeding from heat — converges with the Chinese 止血).

*熟地黃 (prepared):* madhura rasa (predominant); guru-snigdha-picchila (heavy-unctuous-slimy) guṇa; *neutral to slightly uṣṇa vīrya* (the saṃskāra has shifted from cooling to neutral-warming); madhura vipāka. Pacifies Vāta strongly; aggravates Kapha (the heaviness). _Rasāyana_ (rejuvenative — the Chinese 補腎益精 register), _balya_ (strengthening), _bṛṃhaṇa_ (anabolic), _vājīkara_ (mild). The closest Sanskrit-tradition analog would be a deeply-prepared milk-and-honey *rasāyana* preparation; the Chinese 熟地黃 sits in a constitutional register that the Ayurvedic tradition reads through compound preparations rather than single drugs.

The reasoned placement is consistent with the modern phytochemistry but is not classical citation.

=== Viruddha

- *(F) Conditional:* heavy phlegm-damp constitution; weak digestion (paralleling the classical Chinese Spleen-vacuity caution).
- *(M) Modern:*
  - *Hypoglycemic interaction:* rehmannia (both forms) has documented hypoglycemic activity; theoretical interaction with antidiabetic medications.
  - *Anticoagulant theoretical interaction.*
  - *Cardiac glycoside (digitalis-like) trace compounds*: rehmannia is in the foxglove family historically; trace cardiac-glycoside activity has been documented in some preclinical studies, though clinically modest at standard doses.
  - *Pregnancy:* generally avoided at high therapeutic doses.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Rehmannia is_ not classical in Tibb. _The species is exclusively East Asian and was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world; no Avicennan, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or al-Rāzī attestation. Modern Arabic-Persian-Urdu commercial usage is the transliteration_ rīhmāniyya / di huang (ريهمانية / دي هوانغ). _Reasoned modern Unani placement on the Tibb humoral axis (entirely interpretive):_

*Mizāj* of 生地黃 (raw): *Cold and Moist in the 2nd–3rd degree* — strongly cooling; *mubarriḍ jiddan*, *muraṭṭib* (humectant), *qābiḍ* (binding in bleeding), suitable for *amrāḍ ṣafrāwiyya* (bilious / hot-pattern diseases).

*Mizāj* of 熟地黃 (prepared): *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* — the saṃskāra transformation reads as a shift from cold to warm; *muqawwī al-jasad*, *muraṭṭib*, *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac), *muḥsin al-dam* (blood-improving), *muqawwī al-kalā wa-l-kabid* (kidney and liver tonic). Sits in the Tibb register of *muraṭṭib mughadhdhī* (humectant-nourishing) tonics analogous to date-syrup-based and milk-based rasāyana-class preparations.

The substance does not appear in Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Khazāyin al-Adwiya. Modern South-Asian Unani manufacturer products may use it as an imported Chinese-medicine ingredient in compound polyherbals, but the substance has no medieval Tibb tradition. See _tibb-al-arabi.typ_ for the framework.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

地黃 is in countless formulas. Representative examples:

*With 生地黃 (raw):*
- *四物湯 (Sì Wù Tāng, "Four Substances Decoction")* — 熟地黃 (typically) or 生地黃 + 白芍 + 當歸 + 川芎; the foundational blood-tonifying / blood-regulating formula; basis of menstrual-regulation Chinese gynaecology.
- *清營湯 (Qīng Yíng Tāng)* — Wen Bing ying-level formula; 生地黃 + 玄參 + 麥門冬 + 黃連 + 銀花 + 連翹 + 丹參 + 竹葉 + 犀角. From Wu Jutong's *Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn* (1798).
- *增液湯 (Zēng Yè Tāng, "Increase the Fluids Decoction")* — 生地黃 + 麥門冬 + 玄參; for fluid-depletion constipation in Wen Bing.
- *犀角地黃湯 (Xī Jiǎo Dì Huáng Tāng)* — for severe ying-and-blood-level heat with bleeding.

*With 熟地黃 (prepared):*
- *六味地黃丸 (Liù Wèi Dì Huáng Wán, "Six-Ingredient Rehmannia Pill")* — *the most-prescribed Chinese formula globally*; foundational Liver-Kidney-yin tonic. See above for derivatives.
- *知柏地黃丸 (Zhī Bǎi Dì Huáng Wán)* — with anemarrhena and phellodendron; for yin-vacuity with empty-heat (typical symptom: hot-flashes, night sweats, low-grade afternoon fever).
- *杞菊地黃丸 (Qǐ Jú Dì Huáng Wán)* — with goji berry and chrysanthemum; for Liver-Kidney yin-vacuity with eye symptoms.
- *麥味地黃丸 (Mài Wèi Dì Huáng Wán)* — with ophiopogon and schisandra; for Lung-and-Kidney-yin vacuity with chronic cough.
- *桂附地黃丸 / 金匱腎氣丸 (Guì Fù Dì Huáng Wán / Jīn Guì Shèn Qì Wán)* — with cinnamon and aconite; the "Kidney qi pill" for Kidney-yang vacuity. From _Jīnguì Yàolüè_.
- *左歸丸 (Zuǒ Guī Wán)* and *右歸丸 (Yòu Guī Wán)* — Zhang Jingyue's "Restore the Left" / "Restore the Right" pills; left for yin-vacuity, right for yang-vacuity.

*Standard doses:* 9–30 g 生地黃; 9–30 g 熟地黃 in decoction; smaller in pill formulation. The Chinese pharmacy uses substantial doses by Western standards.#footnote[Formula sources: 四物湯 from _Tàipíng Huìmín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_; 六味地黃丸 from Qian Yi, _Xiǎo Ér Yào Zhèng Zhí Jué_ (1119); 桂附地黃丸 from _Jīnguì Yàolüè_; 清營湯 and Wen Bing formulas from Wu Jutong, _Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn_ (1798); 左歸丸 / 右歸丸 from Zhang Jingyue's _Jǐng Yuè Quán Shū_ (1624). Compilation: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas. Modern adoption in Indian Chinese-medicine-influenced practice is minimal and post-classical.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No classical European tradition. Rehmannia reached Western herbal practice only in the late 20th c. as a Chinese-medicine import; modern Western herbal use is post-classical adoption. There is no Hildegard, Dioscorides, or Pliny attestation.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

No classical Tibb tradition; see the reasoned modern placement above.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Rehmannia is principally a *continental-seasonal* (temperate continental, with hot summers and cold winters) substance, with cultivation concentrated in the Huaiqing region of Henan and broader north-central China. See _climates.typ_ for the climate framework.

*Continental-seasonal (Huaiqing 懷慶 / northern Henan — the canonical region).* The climate carries hot wet summers, cold dry winters, and a substantial seasonal swing; the rehmannia plant produces the thickened root tuber as a winter-dormancy storage organ, accumulating the iridoid glycosides (catalpol and related) and other actives as cold-and-drought tolerance compounds. In _ṛtucaryā_ terms the annual cycle is dominated by Vāta-Kapha winter peaks with Pitta-Kapha summer load; in 中醫 terms the climate features 寒燥 (winter cold-dryness) on the Kidney-Lung and 暑濕 (summer summer-heat-damp) on the Spleen-Heart; in Tibb terms the constitutional load shifts seasonally between sawdā' (winter melancholic) and ṣafrā' (summer bilious). *The traditional response and the four-drug-form distinction are precisely climate-coupled*: 生地黃's cooling action addresses summer-Pitta / 暑 heat; 熟地黃's warming-yin-tonifying action addresses winter-Vāta / 寒 cold and depletion. The four-form differentiation gives the practitioner the entire constitutional spectrum from one plant.

*Application across other Chinese climates:* rehmannia is exported as dried 生地黃 and 熟地黃 throughout China and is used universally; the *form-selection itself accommodates the climate* — southern damp-heat climates favor 生地黃 (the cooling form) and northern dry-cold climates favor 熟地黃 (the warming form). This is the classical climate-coupling principle of Chinese pharmacy at work.

*Modern transposition (deśa-viruddha, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Rehmannia exported globally as Chinese-medicine herb reaches every climate; the *form-selection principle* travels well — the same logic applies (生地黃 for hot-dry climates with Pitta-equivalent presentation; 熟地黃 for cool-damp climates with Vāta-equivalent depletion). The Liu Wei Di Huang Wan formula has become the most-prescribed Chinese formula globally precisely because the Liver-Kidney-yin-vacuity pattern it addresses is climate-independent (a constitutional / aging pattern, not a climatic correction). High-dose long-course 熟地黃 in hot-arid climates may aggravate the Kapha-equivalent damp-loading; in cool-damp climates the same dose is constitutionally aligned. The *commercial standardization* of Liu Wei Di Huang Wan in modern Chinese pharmacy makes the substance universally accessible; this is one of the cleaner cases of pre-modern formula reaching global-clinical use.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Iridoid glycosides* — *catalpol* (the principal active, especially abundant in 生地黃), *aucubin*, *leonuride*, *ajugol*, *rehmannioside* A–D; the principal medicinal-claim compound class.
- *Phenethyl glycosides* — *acteoside (verbascoside)*, *isoacteoside*, *cistanoside* F; the antioxidant fraction.
- *Polysaccharides* — substantial; the immunomodulatory fraction, particularly emphasized in modern Chinese pharmacology research.
- *Sugars* — substantial; sucrose, fructose, glucose; *increase dramatically with 熟地黃 preparation* (the Maillard browning and starch hydrolysis during steaming).
- *Amino acids* — substantial; γ-aminobutyric acid (GABA) and arginine notable.
- *5-hydroxymethylfurfural (HMF)* — *characteristic of 熟地黃*, produced by Maillard browning during the steaming process; serves as a quality-control marker for the prepared form.

*The 生 → 熟 transformation chemistry — biochemically vindicated:* the steaming-and-wine-treating process *dramatically alters* the chemistry:
- *Catalpol content decreases substantially* (from ~3–8% in 生地黃 to ~0.1–1% in 熟地黃) — the cooling-clearing principal active is reduced.
- *Polysaccharide content increases* and is modified — the tonifying-nutritive fraction is enhanced.
- *HMF and other Maillard products are generated* — the characteristic dark color, sweet flavor, and warming character.
- *Free amino acids and reducing sugars rise* — the deeply nutritive register.

The classical "saṃskāra-creates-different-drug" doctrine has clean modern chemical correlates: the prepared form genuinely is a different chemical substance from the raw root.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytomedicine_, _Phytochemistry_; substantial published surveys of catalpol / acteoside / polysaccharide / HMF content in 生地黃 vs. 熟地黃. The 生 → 熟 transformation chemistry is well-documented and is the modern correlate of the classical drug-form doctrine.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Hypoglycemic*: catalpol has documented hypoglycemic activity in preclinical models; modest clinical evidence for type-2 diabetes support. The Chinese classical use for _xiāokě_ (consumption-thirst, the diabetic-spectrum syndrome) is mechanistically supported.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: catalpol and acteoside have substantial preclinical anti-inflammatory activity.
- *Immunomodulatory*: the polysaccharide fraction is well-characterized; modest clinical evidence.
- *Hepatoprotective*: preclinical evidence.
- *Antioxidant*: substantial.
- *Anti-osteoporotic / bone-supportive*: 熟地黃 has documented effects on osteoblast activity and bone density in preclinical studies; the modern correlate of the classical 益精填髓 (augments essence and fills the marrow) action.
- *Cardiovascular*: modest effects on cardiac function in preclinical studies; some traces of cardiac-glycoside-like activity from the iridoid fraction; clinical significance modest.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the classical four-form distinction maps cleanly onto modern phytochemistry — 生地黃's high-catalpol-low-polysaccharide cooling-clearing profile is biochemically vindicated as anti-inflammatory and hypoglycemic; 熟地黃's low-catalpol-high-polysaccharide warming-tonifying profile is vindicated as immunomodulatory and tonic-nutritive. The classical pharmacology and the modern chemistry agree.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Traditional Chinese dose*: 9–30 g 生地黃 or 熟地黃 in decoction; smaller (3–9 g) in pill formulation.
- *Liu Wei Di Huang Wan*: standard commercial dose ~8 g of compressed pills 2–3 times daily (typical adult Chinese-medicine pharmacy).
- *Risk groups (use cautiously)*: (a) concurrent *antidiabetic* medication (additive hypoglycemic effect); (b) heavy *Spleen-vacuity-damp* / IBS-type loose stools (the cloying nature); (c) concurrent *anticoagulants* (theoretical); (d) *pregnancy* at high therapeutic dose; (e) *acute febrile conditions* in the case of 熟地黃 (which is warming and tonifying — inappropriate in acute heat).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_. The Liver-Kidney-yin-vacuity constitutional pattern that Liu Wei Di Huang Wan addresses is one of the most-common patterns in modern Chinese-medicine clinical practice; the formula's global use reflects this.]

